layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | The factory builds
    strong packages
    | : one coherent piece of work that one worker can finish in a sitting and that can be integrated on its own. Planning turns a goal ("CSV export for invoices", a spec document) into such packages, with their acceptance criteria and dependencies. There are three ways to do it.
  h2#planner Ask the planner
  ul
    li
      | On a project's page,
      strong Plan new work
      | takes a goal and optional notes. An
      a(href="/docs/operators") operator
      | bot can do the same with
      code plan_request {project, goal, notes}
      | .
    li
      | The
      strong planner
      | is a read-only
      code claude -p
      | session on the project's runner, in the main checkout, with the line's
      code models.planner
      | , a turn limit and a budget. It reads the context files, the existing package specs and the code the goal touches.
    li
      | It answers with a short summary, the packages (local id, title, spec, acceptance criteria, dependencies, sensitive flag, priority) and its questions: what it needs to know, or what it assumed and you should confirm. When the goal is too vague, it returns only questions.
  p
    | What it aims for: packages that each deliver one thing and leave the base branch working; criteria a black-box test can check through the public surface, including refusals (who is not allowed, which input is rejected); criteria marked _(G1)_ when the build and test command proves them and _(G3)_ when only a review can; real dependencies only, so independent packages run in parallel; and fewer, well-cut packages, because each one pays for a full run of the gates.
  h2#approval Approving a plan
  p
    | The planner's packages are created in
    strong Backlog
    | as one plan, and one decision reaches your phone with the summary. You can:
  dl
    dt
      | Approve
    dd
      | Every package of the plan becomes Gata (ready). With autopilot on, they start by themselves as their dependencies allow.
    dt
      | Discard
    dd
      | The plan's packages are deleted.
    dt
      | Edit first
    dd
      | Leave the decision open, change titles, criteria or dependencies on the packages' pages (they are ordinary Backlog packages), then approve.
  h2#skill Plan in Claude Code
  p
    | In a Claude Code session in the repository,
    code /buildyboy:factory-plan <goal or path to a spec>
    | does the planner's job with you in the conversation: it shows you the cut (ids, titles, dependencies) before writing, writes one Markdown file per package under the line's
    code paths.packages
    | , and imports them into the project (
    a(href="/docs/skills#factory-plan") Skills
    | ). The files stay in the repository, so the specs are versioned with the code. Imported this way, the packages keep the status their files say (Backlog by default): move them to Gata on the board when you are happy with them.
  h2#spec Writing package files yourself
  p
    | A package file is Markdown with frontmatter.
    code /buildyboy:factory-init
    | puts a template at
    code factory/package-template.md
    | , outside the packages folder so it is never imported itself:
  pre
    code
      | ---
      | id: P4
      | title: Export invoices as CSV
      | status: backlog
      | depends_on: [P2]
      | priority: 50
      | sensitive: false
      | ---
      | 
      | # P4: Export invoices as CSV
      | 
      | ## Scope
      | ## Sources
      | ## External effects
      | ## What it delivers
      | ## Acceptance criteria
      | 
      | 1. The invoices page has an Export button that downloads the filtered list as CSV.
      | 2. A user without the accounting role gets 403 from the export URL.
      | 3. _(G3)_ The export never includes deleted invoices.
      | 
      | ## Pages for the visual check
  p
    | Then import the folder into the project:
  pre
    code
      | node cli/bb.ts tasks import factory/packages --project shop-api --dry-run
      | BUILDYBOY_AGENT_TOKEN=bba_… node cli/bb.ts tasks import factory/packages --project shop-api
  ul
    li
      | The criteria come from the section headed
      strong Acceptance criteria
      | : every list item is one criterion.
      code --dry-run
      | prints each package with its criteria, numbered, and writes nothing.
    li
      | Ids are local: with the project's prefix
      code SH-
      | ,
      code P4
      | is stored as
      code SH-P4
      | , and
      code depends_on: [P2]
      | means
      code SH-P2
      | .
    li
      | Re-importing updates packages by id and never changes the status of one already started. See
      a(href="/docs/backlog#markdown") Importing Markdown files
      | for every frontmatter key.
